# Read for `docker/Dockerfile` only — BuildKit prefers `<dockerfile>.dockerignore` over the
# context root's. The two applications keep their own beside their own Dockerfile; this one
# exists because this build's context is the repository root, which is where everything
# that is not an input to it also lives.

# The host's own toolchains. The darwin trees are not merely weight: `COPY apps/web/ ./`
# would lay a darwin-arm64 node_modules over the linux one installed a layer earlier, and
# `build/` would hand Gradle an answer from another machine.
#
# Named by path and not as `**/node_modules` and `**/.next`, which is what they were until
# `v0.1.0` failed on them. Those globs match at every depth, so they also emptied
# `dist/web/node_modules` and `dist/web/.next` — the prebuilt bundle the release hands to
# `web-prebuilt` — from the inside, while the note at the foot of this file correctly said
# `dist/` itself must not be excluded. The image built, because a Dockerfile cannot tell
# that a `COPY` arrived short, and died on `Cannot find module 'next'` four minutes later
# inside the smoke test. Anything added here that should not reach `dist/` gets a path too.
node_modules
apps/*/node_modules
apps/web/.next
apps/api/build
apps/api/.gradle
apps/api/.kotlin

# The generated AES key. It belongs to a volume, never to an image layer.
apps/api/data

# Not inputs. `.env` matters most: it is the one file a COPY could bake a secret out of.
**/.env
**/.env.local
.git
.claude
docs
e2e
site
test-results
playwright-report

# `dist/` is deliberately absent: it is where `release.yml` puts the prebuilt jar and Next
# bundle for the api-prebuilt and web-prebuilt stages, and excluding it would empty them.
